{
  "id": 530994271,
  "iid": 26465,
  "project_id": 7898047,
  "title": "blf: reject zero-length compressed containers to prevent divide-by-zero",
  "description": "Summary:\n\nWhen parsing compressed Vector BLF capture files, data_length was passed to g_try_malloc() under the assumption that it returns NULL when data_length is 0. Because g_try_malloc(0) returns a non-NULL pointer in GLib, a compressed container with data_length == 0 bypassed allocation checks and was passed into blf_decompress_zlib() with compressed_len == 0.\n\nIn blf_decompress_zlib(), ws_assert(compressed_len != 0) aborted the process in debug builds (CWE-617), while in release builds without assertions, computing decompressed_len / compressed_len crashed the application with a fatal division-by-zero (SIGFPE, CWE-369).\n\nChanges:\n\n\\- Explicitly check for data_length == 0 in blf_pull_logcontainer_into_memory() and return WTAP_ERR_BAD_FILE before attempting to allocate or decompress.\n\n\\- Replace the reachable ws_assert in blf_decompress_zlib() with a clean WTAP_ERR_BAD_FILE return to protect against division by zero.",
  "state": "merged",
  "created_at": "2026-09-11T12:28:20.114Z",
